establishment, a consequence of the earlier Lê decline."}, {"sourceSlug": "lê-uy-mục", "sourceName": "Lê Uy Mục", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "thăng-long-vietnam", "targetName": "Thăng Long, Đại Việt", "context": "Lê Uy Mục's reign as emperor was centered in Thăng Long, the imperial capital of Đại Việt, from 1505 to 1509."}], "places": [{"name": "Thăng Long, Đại Việt", "role": "Imperial Capital"}, {"name": "Hanoi, Vietnam", "role": "Modern Location"}, {"name": "Thanh Hóa, Đại Việt", "role": "Lê Dynasty Homeland"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Lê Uy Mục", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "lê-uy-mục"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q842218", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%AA_Uy_M%E1%BB%A5c"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Lê Uy Mục's short, tyrannical reign drastically accelerated the decline of the Later Lê dynasty, directly contributing to a period of profound instability and setting the stage for the Mạc dynasty's usurpation and subsequent fragmentation of Đại Việt.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-30T02:37:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36659839139", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Lê Uy Mục, also called Lê Tuấn (黎濬), was the eighth emperor of the later Lê dynasty of Vietnam. He was the second son of Emperor Lê Hiến Tông and the elder half-brother of his direct predecessor, Emperor Lê Túc Tông.", "newValue": "Lê Uy Mục (黎威穆), also known by his birth name Lê Tuấn (黎濬), was the eighth emperor of the Later Lê dynasty of Đại Việt (Vietnam), reigning from 1505 to 1509. Ascending to the throne at a young age after the premature death of his half-brother Lê Túc Tông, Lê Uy Mục's four-year rule is widely regarded by historians as a period of precipitous decline, marking a stark departure from the relative stability and prosperity of his immediate predecessors. His reign is historically significant as a turning point, accelerating internal decay within the Lê court and paving the way for a protracted era of political instability.

Initially showing some promise, Lê Uy Mục quickly descended into a reign characterized by profound cruelty, excessive indulgence, and severe misgovernance. He notoriously ordered the execution of many officials and relatives, including those who had supported his ascension, such as his maternal grandfather Nguyễn Kính. The emperor became known for his lavish spending, incessant drinking, and capricious punishments, often neglecting state affairs entirely in favor of personal pleasures. This neglect led to widespread corruption, the rise of powerful eunuchs, and a significant deterioration of public administration, alienating both the scholar-gentry class and the general populace.

The destructive nature of Lê Uy Mục's rule had profound and lasting consequences for the Later Lê dynasty. His tyrannical behavior and neglect of governance directly fueled popular discontent and rebellion, weakening the central government's authority and legitimacy. This internal strife and the subsequent power vacuum created conditions ripe for the rise of ambitious regional figures, most notably Mạc Đăng Dung, who would eventually usurp the throne and establish the Mạc dynasty a few decades later. Lê Uy Mục's reign thus stands as a critical antecedent to the fragmentation of Đại Việt into warring factions during the 16th century."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-30T02:37:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36659839139", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1488-05-05, Imperial Citadel of Thang Long"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1488-05-05, Imperial Citadel of Thang Long"}], "newValue": ["The unexpected early death of Emperor Lê Túc Tông in 1505 created an immediate power vacuum and succession crisis.", "Court factionalism and the influence of powerful maternal relatives, such as Nguyễn Kính, played a significant role in Lê Uy Mục's ascension over other potential heirs.", "The entrenched system of imperial succession within the Later Lê dynasty allowed for disputes and the elevation of less suitable candidates under specific political circumstances."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-30T02:37:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36659839139", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1509-12-01"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1509-12-01"}], "newValue